        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "ce41c956-54d5-5a05-9e9f-b7fbf908dbd5",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Neil B. Shulman, Michael Caton-Jones, Jeffrey Price, Peter S. Seaman, Dallas Hall, Elijah Saunders\n- Products: Doc Hollywood, What? Dead...Again?\n- Organizations: International Society on Hypertension in Blacks\n- Topics: Hypertension among African Americans, minority health\n\nFacts:\n- In 1986, physicians Dallas Hall, Neil B. Shulman, and Elijah Saunders founded the International Society on Hypertension in Blacks in response to concern about high rates of hypertension among African Americans.\n- In 1991, the American romantic comedy film Doc Hollywood was directed by Michael Caton-Jones and written by Jeffrey Price and Peter S. Seaman.\n- In 1991, Doc Hollywood was based on Neil B. Shulman’s book What? Dead...Again?\n- Neil B. Shulman was the associate producer of the 1991 film Doc Hollywood, which was based on one of his books.\n- By 2006, the International Society on Hypertension in Blacks had broadened its scope from reducing hypertension among African Americans to improving the health of minority populations around the world.",
